WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.280
Episode 31, I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.800
Today is July 13th, 2026.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.700
From starbase to orbit, here's your SpaceX rundown.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.220
Flight 13's propellant and avionics upgrades

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.980
move Starship one step closer to demonstrating

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.160
the rapid reuse that would collapse launch costs.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.620
Regulators cleared the integrated test flight window

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.000
opening July 16 at starbase.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.860
The mission uses the second V3 Starship and Super Heavy stack.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.340
Tesla Roddy reports a series of hardware and software changes

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.160
from flight 12, including updated propellant management

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.340
and avionics.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.360
These revisions target improved performance

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.280
during the boostback burn and separation sequence.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.280
The launch window opens at 6.30pm ET

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.240
from starbase in South Texas.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.820
This marks the second flight of the V3 vehicles

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.620
and builds directly on flight 12 results.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.880
Listeners should watch for whether the new configuration

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.560
supports the first V3 Starlink deployment attempt.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.200
Starships ongoing flight test campaign at starbase

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.620
keeps booster catch and full reuse as central goals.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.700
Today's clearance advances the open question

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.180
of orbital refueling demonstration timeline

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.620
by testing revised systems in flight.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.980
The vehicle carries revised tank baffles and sensor suites

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.000
to handle higher propellant flow rates.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.840
Updated guidance software aims to tighten the boost back

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:33.380
corridor.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.800
The second V3 stack also incorporates refined heat shield

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.880
attachment points carried over from flight 12 lessons.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.240
These incremental hardware steps directly address

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.600
the open question of booster turnaround time between flights.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.500
The mission remains the second integrated flight test

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.460
with V3 Starship and super heavy vehicles.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.980
Changes focus on propellant management and avionics refinements

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.420
that build on prior data.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.580
Starships flight test arc at starbase

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:05.780
continues with these targeted revisions.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.660
Success here would move the reuse cadence goal forward

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.480
by validating higher flow hardware in a real ascent profile.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.280
With flight 13 now confirmed,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.040
we turn to what the upper stage will actually carry.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.520
20 V3 starlink satellites are manifested

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.900
for the flight 13 upper stage.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:28.480
The deployment profile tests new dispenser hardware

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.860
and release sequencing.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.080
Success would mark the first operational use

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.700
of the V3 bus on a Starship mission.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.060
Starlink's constellation growth receives its first V3

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.600
test aboard Starship.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.400
The deployment sequencing data will inform

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.840
how the higher throughput bus integrates

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.860
with existing laser link and direct to cell plans.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.500
While Starship dominates the near term test calendar,

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.240
Falcon 9 keeps the cadence alive on the Florida coast.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.420
A Falcon 9 is scheduled for a late night lift off

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.220
from Cape Canaveral carrying a commercial payload.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.960
The mission continues the high cadence reuse campaign

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.160
on the Florida pad.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.260
Range and weather checks remain the final gates

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.880
before the overnight window.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.960
Falcon 9's reuse counts keep climbing

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.260
with this Florida flight.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.720
The overnight timing tests operational flexibility

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.980
at the Cape while the annual launch rate ceiling question

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.020
stays open for attentive listeners.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.080
As launch rates press against existing infrastructure limits,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:33.300
one study explores an unconventional work around.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.120
Federal agencies are studying conversion

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.740
of decommissioned Gulf of Mexico platforms

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.440
into offshore launch sites.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.140
The concept would add fixed infrastructure

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.600
without new coastal real estate approvals.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.340
Early engineering reviews focus on deck load ratings

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.920
and exhaust plume management for vehicles

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.420
the size of Falcon 9.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.960
Launch infrastructure readiness at existing pads

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:58.540
remains a tracked constraint.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.800
This offshore study explores an alternate path

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.800
that could ease pressure on star base

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.560
and Cape turnaround times

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.420
if deck ratings prove sufficient.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.540
Meanwhile, Starlink continues expanding its ground footprint

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.980
with fresh test data from a new continent.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.980
SpaceX received permission to conduct

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.860
initial gateway station tests in South Africa.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.640
The trials will measure uplink and downlink performance

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.740
ahead of potential service licensing.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.220
Data collected will inform terminal certification

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.420
and spectrum coordination for the African continent.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:35.060
Starlink's constellation expansion

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.240
continues with these gateway tests.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.780
Direct to cell capability ramp

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.680
and subscriber growth questions

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.440
receive fresh performance data

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.320
from the planned South African links.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:49.380
On the AI front, cursor released the sand agent

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.200
from multi-file code editing

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.040
and terminal execution inside its workspace.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:58.000
The early build is being tested against competing agents

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.280
from anthropic and open AI.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:02.840
Integration work with GROC models

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:05.360
continues in parallel with the public rollout.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.040
Cursors position as the point where GROC

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.180
reaches developers gains another tool

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.820
with sand's multi-file and terminal features.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.700
Tech Times noted that sand development

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.800
precedes any major SpaceX roadmap updates

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.720
that could affect compute allocation

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:23.140
between orbital and terrestrial clusters.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.420
The timing keeps developer tooling in focus

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.200
while larger infrastructure questions remain open.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.800
Cursors agent work therefore sits alongside

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.100
ongoing GROC integration testing

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.420
without yet intersecting new orbital compute decisions.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.220
While SpaceX pushes vertical landing,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.620
other teams are testing alternative recovery geometries.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:46.500
Worth keeping an eye on,

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.160
a South Korean team demonstrated net based booster recovery

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.340
on a C platform.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:54.020
The approach uses a different geometry

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.920
from tower arms and targets lower landing precision requirements.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.620
The test provides comparative data

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.140
on structural loads during ocean recovery.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.320
The method reduces touchdown precision demands

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.880
but adds skirt loads that future trials

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.020
must quantify for larger vehicles.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:13.220
From an engineering standpoint,

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:17.120
the central downcomer inside Super Heavy's liquid oxygen tank

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.720
is essentially a Falcon 9 scale methane transfer line,

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:22.600
running the full height of the booster.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:24.840
By routing chilled propellant

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.540
through this large diameter pipe

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.560
rather than relying solely on the upper tank dome,

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:32.920
designers keep the Raptor inlet condition stable

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.620
even when the vehicle is nearly empty of methane.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.940
The pipe's volume adds meaningful propellant mass

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.180
that simplified animations omit,

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.160
directly affecting the true mass fraction available

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.300
for the boost back and landing burns.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.180
Internal volume calculations must therefore include

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:53.360
the downcomer cross section to avoid underestimating

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.220
available methane for the final descent sequence.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.660
This plumbing choice trades added dry mass

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:03.160
and fabrication complexity for operational robustness.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.940
A smaller, lighter downcomer would reduce structural loads

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.720
but risk vapor ingestion or pressure drop

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.660
at the engines during the critical final seconds of flight.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:16.680
The current design therefore accepts a higher idiot index

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.520
on the tank assembly in exchange

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.580
for repeatable engine starts across multiple flights.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.860
The same principle that made Falcon 9 reuse economical

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.800
once the landing legs and grid fins were refined.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:33.460
The downcomer diameter itself becomes a first principles variable,

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:36.780
large enough to maintain flow at low tank levels

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:40.560
yet integrated without compromising the LOX tank's

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:41.900
primary structural path.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.080
If the downcomer can be produced at scale

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:47.300
with minimal rework between flights,

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.520
it lowers the cost per kilogram delivered to orbit

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.200
by enabling the booster to fly again

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.840
with only routine inspections.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.000
The next data point will come from flight 13 telemetry

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.920
on propellant residuals and engine inlet temperatures.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:05.080
Any measurable improvement in landing margins

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.540
would validate keeping the large internal pipe

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:10.860
rather than pursuing a lighter but riskier alternative.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.880
Raptor engine program continuity benefits directly

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.920
since stable inlet conditions reduce the variables

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.720
that must be re-qualified after each landing

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:26.820
and a quick market note SPC X closed at $140.74

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:29.600
